cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "Dimensional Asymptotics of Euler-Based Simulated Likelihood for Multidimensional Diffusions"
abstract: >-
  A widely used simulation method for likelihood inference in discretely observed
  diffusions approximates each transition density by simulating an Euler scheme to one
  step before the observed endpoint and averaging the final Gaussian density. This work
  shows that the endpoint estimator is a shrinking-bandwidth kernel estimator whose
  effective simulation size is S/M^(K/2) rather than S, where K is the state dimension,
  M the number of Euler substeps and S the number of simulations. It derives exact
  Brownian moments, the general local moment law, the corrected density-level
  normalisation, and the pointwise mean squared error, minimised at M ~ S^(2/(K+4)). It
  then gives a direct counterexample to the joint transition-density statements of
  Brandt and Santa-Clara (2002): along M = S, a sequence satisfying their own rate
  condition and regularity assumptions, the simulated density converges in probability
  to zero for every K > 2 although the true density is strictly positive. This refutes
  the consistency asserted in their Lemma 2 and the central-limit statement of their
  Lemma 3, so the proofs of their parameter-estimator theorems fail. Inconsistency of
  the resulting argmax estimator is not proved. In a Gaussian location model the
  opposite holds: the simulated maximiser is consistent along the very sequences on
  which the density collapses, so the counterexample does not transfer. That is proved
  outright in five dimensions and above, where a covering estimate for the criterion's
  envelope makes it square integrable; in three and four dimensions, the latter being
  the dimension of the application, it rests on a uniform-integrability condition for
  the empirical tails which is stated but not verified, and the natural repair there
  yields a rate condition incompatible with the published one. The rate is a separate matter. For the
  nearest-atom proxy criterion the score at the truth and the curvature there are both
  obtained exactly, and they predict that the estimator is not root-N consistent, which
  would contradict the conclusion of the published Lemma 5 rather than its proof; the
  step joining them is confirmed numerically but not established, and is set out
  explicitly. Issues specific to the exchange-rate application of that paper,
  which concern its specification rather than the estimator, are treated in a companion
  note deposited in the same record.
authors:
  - family-names: Ribeiro
    given-names: Diogo
    email: dfr@esmad.ipp.pt
    affiliation: "School of Media Arts and Design, Polytechnic of Porto"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-2022-7072"
keywords:
  - simulated maximum likelihood
  - diffusion processes
  - Euler scheme
  - transition density
  - Monte Carlo variance
  - curse of dimensionality
  - diffusion bridges
  - nonregular inference
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/DiogoRibeiro7/sml_diffusions_paper"
date-released: 2026-08-11
version: v1.9.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21719868
# Zenodo mints a version DOI only when a release is published, and the identifier
# it issues cannot be predicted, so the entry for the release named above is added
# in the first commit after that release. Until then this list ends at the previous
# version. The concept DOI is what the `doi` field carries, and it never changes.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21719868
    description: >-
      Concept DOI. Represents every version of this work and always resolves to
      the most recent one. Cite this unless you need to pin a specific version.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21887801
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.9.0, the current release. It repairs the covering
      lemma, whose density infimum was taken over the parameter set rather than
      over the neighbourhood the union bound requires; the repaired lemma costs
      only a constant, so the unconditional uniform law and the location-model
      consistency corollary survive with their exponent intact. It also demotes
      the score-level corollary, which claimed a necessity its proof withheld,
      supplies the equicontinuity that the step-size uniformity lemma had
      assumed, and renames the numerical table for the nearest-atom proxy it
      actually measures.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21877843
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.8.0, superseded by defect. Its covering lemma is
      false as stated: it takes the atom density's infimum over the parameter
      set and applies it to balls around points of that set, which protrude
      beyond it, so for a Gaussian cloud the claimed lower bound on a ball's
      mass exceeds the mass itself. The covering tail bound therefore fails, and
      with it the proof of the unconditional uniform law and of the consistency
      corollary that rests on it. Both results survive in v1.9.0, whose repaired
      lemma costs only a constant, but the proof given in v1.8.0 does not
      establish them. Its score-level corollary also claims a necessity its own
      proof withholds, and its numerical table measures the nearest-atom proxy
      while naming the simulated maximiser.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21857286
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.7.0, superseded by defect. It states that the
      empirical-tail condition cannot be discharged at K = 3 and K = 4, the
      application's dimension included, and argues the obstruction is
      structural. It is not: the argument holds for truncation on the criterion
      and fails for truncation on the centre, which v1.8.0 uses to make the
      result unconditional in every dimension K > 2. Two antithetic pair
      correlations are also misrounded, and five occurrences of a macro in the
      antithetic section were destroyed by a shell escape and typeset as stray
      italic text.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21855599
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.6.1, superseded by v1.7.0, which proves the uniform
      law of large numbers unconditionally in five dimensions and above and
      establishes uniformity of the interchange in the step size. Nothing in
      v1.6.1 is known to be wrong; its estimator-level results are conditional
      in every dimension where v1.7.0 makes them unconditional above four.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21850110
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.6.0, superseded by defect. Its corrections are sound
      but incompletely applied: having labelled the location-model consistency
      corollary conditional, it still asserts in three places that consistency
      is settled, and carries an orphaned paragraph citing the uniform law as
      available. Its contribution list also still states the score-level rate
      as a necessary condition after the corollary establishing it was
      downgraded. A reader could take either side of the contradiction.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21848464
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.5.0, superseded by defect. Its uniform law of large
      numbers for the nearest-atom criterion is labelled proved, but the proof
      controls the supremum over theta of an expected empirical tail where it
      needs the expectation of the supremum, so the location-model consistency
      corollary that depends on it is not established either. v1.5.0 also
      describes the score and curvature results as concerning the simulated
      score, when they concern the nearest-atom proxy criterion, whose relation
      to the log likelihood is through criterion values rather than
      derivatives. The exact score moment and the curvature identity themselves
      are unaffected and were confirmed by external review.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21834941
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.4.0, superseded by defect. It introduces the uniform
      law of large numbers whose proof v1.6.0 corrects, and states the
      location-model consistency corollary as proved; both are conditional on an
      empirical-tail hypothesis that v1.4.0 does not verify. Its correction of
      the rate condition misreported in v1.3.0 stands.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21830098
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.3.0, superseded. Its remark on the uniform-in-theta
      step asserts that the step plausibly requires N >> S^(2/K) and that this
      points the opposite way to the published rate conditions. Both are wrong:
      the chaining bound was taken over the metric diameter of the index set
      rather than over the envelope of the function class, and the correct
      condition, N >> log S, is compatible with those conditions. No result in
      v1.3.0 depends on the claim, which described an open step rather than
      serving as a hypothesis.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21823961
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.2.0, superseded by v1.3.0, which closes two problems
      v1.2.0 records as open: the logarithmic gap in the subcritical collapse
      theorem, and the dependence of the scaling law on assumption (A5).
      Nothing in v1.2.0 is known to be wrong; its results are weaker.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21763715
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.1.0, superseded by v1.2.0. Nothing in it is known to be
      wrong; it is simply the single-document form.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21760062
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.0.3, superseded by v1.1.0. Its Proposition 3.1 gives the
      critical case without the limit law, and its Section 6 states the score
      result conditionally.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21729471
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.0.2, superseded. Its Proposition E.2 omits the
      hypothesis |rho_ww*| <= 1 and is false as a standalone statement.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21728285
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.0.1, superseded. Its Remark E.6 wrongly calls a
      magnitude condition vacuous.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21719869
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v1.0.0, superseded. It states the limit in Proposition C.1
      in the wrong direction.
references:
